<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cho Man-sik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1883–1950</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cho Man-sik (1883–1950) was a Korean Christian nationalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, known for his non-violent resistance to Japanese rule and his campaign in the 1920s to buy Korean-made goods. After the Japanese surrender in 1945 he led the people's committee of South Pyongan under Soviet occupation, but he opposed the trusteeship plan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, was placed under house arrest in January 1946 and is believed to have been killed during the Korean War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cho-man-sik.jpg" alt="Cho Man-sik" width="240" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cho_Man-sik_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
